# The shared schema: every relation, its columns in order, and what points at what.
#
# **Shared by the state before and the state after**, which is why it is its own file - `before.4x`
# and `expected.4x` differ by one row and neither repeats any of this.
#
# **Every relation's key is its first column**, which is a convention rather than a dependency, so
# there is no `key` relation. Where no natural key existed a surrogate was made, and the dotted
# ones are readable on purpose.


## The structure's own structure
#
# WHY: this section is the self-description. Nothing needs it to run the first test - the engine
# could know these eleven relations. It is here so that the claim *everything is data* is
# checkable rather than asserted, and it is the first thing to cut if that is not worth 56 rows.

{relation name:relation}
{column id:relation.name relation:relation seq:1 name:name}
{relation name:column}
{column id:column.id relation:column seq:1 name:id}
{column id:column.relation relation:column seq:2 name:relation}
{column id:column.seq relation:column seq:3 name:seq}
{column id:column.name relation:column seq:4 name:name}
{reference column:column.relation to:relation}
{relation name:reference}
{column id:reference.column relation:reference seq:1 name:column}
{column id:reference.to relation:reference seq:2 name:to}
{reference column:reference.column to:column}
{reference column:reference.to to:relation}
{relation name:role}
{column id:role.name relation:role seq:1 name:name}
{relation name:rule}
{column id:rule.name relation:rule seq:1 name:name}
{relation name:input}
{column id:input.id relation:input seq:1 name:id}
{column id:input.rule relation:input seq:2 name:rule}
{column id:input.seq relation:input seq:3 name:seq}
{column id:input.name relation:input seq:4 name:name}
{column id:input.of relation:input seq:5 name:of}
{reference column:input.rule to:rule}
{reference column:input.of to:relation}
{relation name:clause}
{column id:clause.id relation:clause seq:1 name:id}
{column id:clause.rule relation:clause seq:2 name:rule}
{column id:clause.seq relation:clause seq:3 name:seq}
{column id:clause.role relation:clause seq:4 name:role}
{column id:clause.relation relation:clause seq:5 name:relation}
{reference column:clause.rule to:rule}
{reference column:clause.role to:role}
{reference column:clause.relation to:relation}
{relation name:binding}
{column id:binding.id relation:binding seq:1 name:id}
{column id:binding.clause relation:binding seq:2 name:clause}
{column id:binding.column relation:binding seq:3 name:column}
{column id:binding.input relation:binding seq:4 name:input}
{reference column:binding.clause to:clause}
{reference column:binding.column to:column}
{reference column:binding.input to:input}
{relation name:command}
{column id:command.id relation:command seq:1 name:id}
{column id:command.rule relation:command seq:2 name:rule}
{reference column:command.rule to:rule}
{relation name:argument}
{column id:argument.id relation:argument seq:1 name:id}
{column id:argument.command relation:argument seq:2 name:command}
{column id:argument.input relation:argument seq:3 name:input}
{column id:argument.value relation:argument seq:4 name:value}
{reference column:argument.command to:command}
{reference column:argument.input to:input}

{relation name:state}
{column id:state.relation relation:state seq:1 name:relation}
{reference column:state.relation to:relation}


## Which relations are the game's state
#
# **This is what bounds the comparison.** `expected.4x` states the state after, and a diff has to
# know which relations to look at - without this it would compare the rule and the command too,
# and they are in the actual because they were loaded rather than because anything happened.
#
# WHY: four rows saying what a reader can already see. The alternative is for the comparison to
# scope itself to whatever relations `expected.4x` happens to mention, which is the same thing
# decided by omission - and a relation left out of `expected.4x` would then go unchecked silently.

{state relation:territory}
{state relation:thing}
{state relation:adjacency}
{state relation:residency}


## The game's structure, which is Sean's four tables

{relation name:territory}
{column id:territory.id relation:territory seq:1 name:id}
{relation name:thing}
{column id:thing.id relation:thing seq:1 name:id}
{column id:thing.name relation:thing seq:2 name:name}
{relation name:adjacency}
{column id:adjacency.from relation:adjacency seq:1 name:from}
{column id:adjacency.to relation:adjacency seq:2 name:to}
{reference column:adjacency.from to:territory}
{reference column:adjacency.to to:territory}
{relation name:residency}
{column id:residency.what relation:residency seq:1 name:what}
{column id:residency.where relation:residency seq:2 name:where}
{reference column:residency.what to:thing}
{reference column:residency.where to:territory}


